course: dictionary
lesson: 6379
title: Plant words and the clues around them
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name things you can see outside, like plants. When you meet a plant word you do not know, look at what the text says about it. «Mugwort is a tall wild plant with grey-green, deeply cut leaves» tells you what it looks like. The name is the new word, and the description is the clue.
explain: The text also tells you where it grows and how it smells. Mugwort grows on roadsides and waste ground, and it has a strong smell. If you saw it on a walk, these clues would help you know it is mugwort and not a grass or a bush. Ask yourself: what kind of thing is this, and what does the text say it looks like or does?
explain: Some clues are about the past. People once used mugwort in herbal medicine and to flavour beer, and in old folk tales it was thought to protect travellers. A word can tell you more than how a plant looks: it can also tell you how people used it long ago.
explain: The example sentence «Chornobyl is the name of a kind of mugwort, a wormwood plant» shows that two plant names can mean the same thing. Another sentence says a «coincidence» was noticed, which means two things matched by chance. Read the words around a new word, and check that your meaning still makes sense in the sentence.
fact: Mugwort is a tall wild plant with grey-green, deeply cut leaves.
fact: The leaves are silvery underneath, and the plant has a strong smell.
fact: Mugwort grows on roadsides and waste ground in Britain, Ireland and North America.
fact: People once used mugwort in herbal medicine and to flavour beer.
fact: «Coincidence» means two things happening together by chance.
exercise: «Mugwort has grey-green, deeply cut leaves.» What colour are the leaves? | Sky blue | Bright red | Pure white | *Grey-green
exercise: Where does mugwort grow, according to the lesson? | In deep rivers | On the tops of high mountains | *On roadsides and waste ground | Inside people's houses
exercise: Why might mugwort make some people sneeze? | Its leaves are very hot to touch | It only grows in the sea | It is a kind of sweet cake | *Its pollen can cause hay fever
test: What does «coincidence» mean? | A planned party | A type of weather | A kind of plant | *Something that happens by chance
test: Mugwort was once used to flavour what? | Bread | *Beer | Soup | Milk
exam: What should you do with a word you do not know? | Stop reading and give up | Guess the first letter only | *Read the words around it for clues and check your meaning fits | Skip the whole text for ever
